// ============================================================================
// review.bx — `burxt review old.bx new.bx`, written in Burxt. The counterpart of
// `src/rust-compiler/review.rs`.
//
// **Why this file has to exist at all**, and it is not "for symmetry".
// `spec/1.0/ROADMAP-1.0.md` THE GATE states the rule this whole directory serves:
//
//   > Rust may BUILD Burxt. Burxt may not USE Rust.
//
// and then names this row as the sharpest case of it. §C2 makes `burxt review` the
// **mechanical semver rule** for the 1.0 compatibility promise — the tool that decides
// whether a release may call itself compatible. While it lives only in Rust, *Burxt
// cannot enforce its own compatibility promise without Rust*, which is exactly what the
// rule forbids. Every other Rust-only module is a capability Burxt has not written yet;
// this one was a dependency in the release process.
//
// ---- what it reports -------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Not a diff. A diff shows every line that moved; this shows only the lines that changed a
// GUARANTEE, and names which guarantee. The claim is narrow and checkable:
//
//   > A change that alters no signature cannot alter what the program promises.
//
// Declarations only — the lexer and the parser, and **deliberately not the checker**. Three
// consequences, all wanted: it is fast; it does not care whether the bodies are finished;
// and it cannot be confused by a reformat, because it compares meanings rather than text.
//
// Not running the checker is also what keeps this byte-identical with `review.rs`, and that
// is worth writing down rather than relying on silently. `declared_type` turns a bare
// interface name into `dynamic Priced` — but only by asking `self.types`, and `check` is what
// fills that table in. So an unchecked Unit renders `Priced`, and stage-0, which learns the
// same fact in its own typechecker, renders `Priced` too. Calling `check` here to be "more
// informative" would break the agreement in one line, on a program with an interface in a
// signature.
//
// ---- exit codes, because this is meant to be a gate -----------------------------------
//
// `0` nothing weakened · `1` something weakened · `2` a file could not be read or parsed.
// So `burxt review a.bx b.bx` in CI needs no output parsing.
//
// ---- where this does NOT yet agree with `review.rs`, stated plainly -------------------
//
// 1. **A file that does not parse.** stage-1's lexer and parser report through `print`,
//    which is stdout; `review.rs` fails with a `Result` and `main.rs` prints one line to
//    stderr. Both exit 2, and the exit code is what a gate reads — but the TEXT on a
//    malformed input is not the same, and cannot be without changing how `ast.bx` and
//    `parser.bx` report. Named here rather than hidden, because the agreement claim is
//    about programs that parse.
// 2. **A file that does not EXIST**, which is a sharper version of 1 and the one worth acting
//    on. `review.rs` exits **2** with `error: cannot read <path>`; this exits **70**, because
//    `read_file` is total in Rust and ABORTS in Burxt — there is no `file_exists` builtin, so
//    `load_program` cannot look before it leaps. Both are non-zero and both say what happened,
//    so nothing is silent; but a CI gate written as `if code == 2` would read them differently,
//    and §C2 is about a CI gate. Measured, not reasoned about: `burxt review /nope.bx x.bx`
//    answers 2 and the harness answers 70.
//
//    The fix is one line and it is deliberately NOT taken here, because it is a decision about
//    the compiler's link surface rather than about this file:
//
//        external function access(path: String, mode: CInt) -> CInt touches files;
//
//    with `access(path, 4) != 0` meaning unreadable. That would make `review.bx` the first
//    compiler module to name a foreign symbol — `lib/os.bx` does it, the compiler never has —
//    and every build of `main.bx` would carry it once `main.bx` uses this file. A `file_exists`
//    builtin, or a `read_file` that can answer "no", is the better shape and is a language
//    change. So: reported, with the code, for whoever owns that call.
// 3. **A string literal inside a contract clause.** `review.rs` renders one with Rust's
//    `{:?}`, which decodes the escapes and re-escapes them Rust's way; this renders the
//    source span verbatim. Identical for every literal whose escapes Rust spells the same
//    way, and different for Burxt's own `\{` and `\}` — which decode to `{` and `}` and
//    would come back out unescaped. A clause containing a braced string literal is the
//    only program that can tell the two apart.
//
// 1 and 3 are FALSE POSITIVES in the direction that matters: they can make a comparison report
// a change that changed nothing, never hide one. `review.rs` records the same preference for the
// same reason — "a false positive is noise, but a false negative is a weakened contract nobody
// was told about." 2 is neither direction: it is a wrong EXIT CODE on an input that is not a
// comparison at all, which is why it is the one with a fix attached.
//
// And one both tools share, found by measuring them against each other rather than by reading
// either: **`item: Priced` and `item: dynamic Priced` are the SAME type** — v0.0.155 made the
// bare interface name mean the dynamic one — and both implementations report
// `CHANGED (Priced) -> Int -> (dynamic Priced) -> Int` when a signature is rewritten from one
// spelling to the other. That is a review crying wolf on a reformat, which is the one thing
// `reformatted` exists to prove it does not do. It is NOT a disagreement between the two, so it
// is not this file's to fix unilaterally; the fix is for both to render kind 46 and kind 49 the
// same way when the name is an interface's, which needs the type table — see above for why that
// is not free. Written down because a defect neither implementation can see alone is exactly
// what a second implementation is for.
//
// ---- what it depends on, and one dependency that is a surprise ------------------------
//
// This file was written meaning NOT to use `emit.bx`: a review reads two signatures and
// emits nothing, so pulling in four thousand lines of code generator looked like exactly the
// wrong layering — `parser.bx` and `check.bx` do not depend on the backend either.
//
// **That is not a choice this file gets to make.** `Unit` has a field `slots: [Slot]`, and
// `Slot` is declared in `emit.bx` — so anything that can spell `Unit` already depends on the
// backend, and `use "ast.bx"` alone does not compile. Reported rather than worked around:
// `Slot` belongs in `ast.bx` with every other shape, and moving it is a one-line change
// that would let `review.bx`, `schema.bx` and any future tool read an AST without linking a
// code generator. Until then the dependency is real and pretending otherwise in a comment
// would be the worse of the two.
//
// Having lost that argument, the right answer is to USE what is now in scope, and two things
// here are better for it: `clause_text` and `replace_whole_word` in `emit.bx` are the clause
// spelling the RUNTIME message uses, so quoting a clause through them means a contract's
// failure message and its review name the same bytes by construction rather than by two
// implementations staying in step. All this file adds is stripping the keyword back off —
// see `without_clause_keyword`.
//
// `lib/` is still not a dependency, like every other compiler module. `lib/string.bx` has
// `string_find` and friends, and the compiler deliberately does not use the standard library,
// so the three small string helpers here are written locally.
// ============================================================================

use "ast.bx";
use "lexer.bx";
use "parser.bx";
use "check.bx";
use "modules.bx";
use "emit.bx";

// ---- the verdicts ---------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// `review.rs` makes these five `const &str` and now so does this file. Until A2 they were
// five one-line `pure` functions, with a comment here saying *"Burxt has no `const` yet"* —
// a workaround that named its own blocker, which is why it was the first thing converted
// when the blocker went away. A limitation left behind after it stops being true is worse
// than a missing feature: nobody re-tests what the comments say does not work.
//
// They also carry their own weight as the dogfood for a STRING const specifically. The
// `INT_MAX`/`INT_MIN` pair in `check.bx` proves the Int path through the fixpoint; these
// five prove the String path, where the emitter interns the literal from the source rather
// than reading a folded number.
//
// WEAKENED means "this promises LESS than it did", and it is the only verdict that should
// stop a review. Everything else is information.
const WEAKENED: String = "WEAKENED";
const STRICTER: String = "STRICTER";
const ADDED: String = "ADDED";
const REMOVED: String = "REMOVED";
const CHANGED: String = "CHANGED";

// Weakenings first. A reviewer reads the top of the output.
pure function verdict_rank(verdict: String) -> Int {
    if verdict == "WEAKENED" { return 0; }
    if verdict == "CHANGED" { return 1; }
    if verdict == "REMOVED" { return 2; }
    if verdict == "STRICTER" { return 3; }
    return 4;
}

// ---- what one declaration promises ----------------------------------------------------

// Everything one declaration promises, flattened into something comparable.
//
// `review.rs` keeps a `BTreeMap<String, Promise>` keyed by qualified name — `Account`,
// `Account.balance`, `Account.withdraw`. Burxt has no map, so this is an array sorted by
// `name` once collection is done, which delivers the same two things the BTreeMap was
// there for: a lookup, and a STABLE output order, so a reviewer reading two runs is not
// also diffing the order.
//
// The clauses are not fields here, and that is the one place this is not a transcription.
// `review.rs` stores `Vec<String>` per promise, each entry being the comparable shape and
// the display text joined by U+0001 and split apart again at every use. Two side arrays
// with a start and a count say the same thing with no separator — the shape stage-1's own
// arena uses for every child list — and it removes the one input that could break the Rust
// version, namely a clause whose own text contains U+0001.
class Promise {
    name: String,
    // `function`, `method`, `constructor`, `class`, `field`, `interface`, `const`.
    kind: String,
    // The parameter and return types, rendered. Not the body, and not the parameter NAMES —
    // renaming a parameter changes no promise, and reporting it would be noise that teaches
    // a reviewer to stop reading.
    shape: String,
    requires_at: Int,
    requires_count: Int,
    ensures_at: Int,
    ensures_count: Int,
    is_pure: Int,
    // `private` is a KEYWORD here — it appears where a field name is expected, in
    // `private balance: Decimal<2>`, so the lexer reserves it and a field cannot be called
    // that. `review.rs` names this field `private`; this one cannot, and being unable to
    // spell it is the language keeping its own rule rather than an inconvenience.
    is_private: Int,
    // What this reaches outside itself, as the bitmask `parse_touches` already built: bit 1
    // files, 2 commands, 4 clock, 8 input, 16 network, 32 model.
    //
    // GAINING one is the second half of what a reviewer needs — "this function now talks to
    // the network" is a change in what the program can DO, and since v0.0.159 it cannot
    // happen without the signature changing.
    //
    // A mask where `review.rs` has a `Vec<Effect>`, and the two orders agree for a reason
    // rather than by luck: `parse_touches` in `parser.rs` ends with `effects.sort()`, and
    // `Effect`'s derived `Ord` is its declaration order, which is the order the bits are
    // numbered in. So iterating bits low to high visits the effects in exactly the order
    // the Rust `Vec` holds them, and the findings come out in the same order. It also
    // refuses a duplicate, so nothing is lost by a set.
    touches: Int,
}

// Every promise a file makes, plus the clause tables the promises index into.
//
// **It is FILLED rather than returned**, which is the shape the region rule chose. A class
// holding growable arrays cannot be a return type — *"cannot return Promises, because its
// storage lives in a region and would not outlive it"* — and `allocates` does not lift that;
// the value would be built in the callee's frame. So the caller opens one and the callee is
// handed it `mutable`, which is what `M1`'s escape rule asks for and reads better anyway: the
// two files' promise tables are visibly the caller's, side by side, rather than two returns
// that happen not to share anything. `lsp.bx` hit the identical wall on `Unit` in the same
// version.
class Promises {
    promises: [Promise],
    // Parallel: the comparable SHAPE of each clause, and the text to SHOW. Two arrays and
    // not an array of pairs, because a pair would be a class copied at every comparison.
    shapes: [String],
    texts: [String],
}

// One thing worth telling a reviewer, and how much it should worry them.
class Finding { verdict: String, name: String, detail: String }

// ---- string helpers, written here for the reason the header gives ---------------------

// `{:<width}` from `review.rs`'s `println!`: pad on the right, and never truncate.
//
// Rust's formatter counts CHARACTERS and this counts bytes, and they cannot disagree here:
// the two padded columns are a verdict from the closed list above and a declaration's
// qualified name, and `is_alpha` in `ast.bx` admits only ASCII into an identifier. A
// non-ASCII name is unspellable, so there is no input that separates the two counts.
pure function review_pad(text: String, width: Int) -> String {
    let mutable out: String = text;
    let mutable i: Int = len(text);
    while i < width {
        out = out + " ";
        i += 1;
    }
    return out;
}

// The clause text with its leading `requires`/`ensures`/`decreases` word taken back off.
//
// `clause_text` in `emit.bx` is what quotes a clause, and it is reused here on purpose — see
// the header: the runtime failure message and the review then name the same bytes because
// they came from the same function, not because two implementations agree today. But that
// function is building a message that has to say `requires`, and a finding here reads
// `` lost `requires amount <= self.balance` `` and puts the word back itself. So the word
// comes off, and this is the whole of the difference between the two callers.
//
// Stripping is by shape rather than by measuring one of three keyword lengths: the first run
// of letters, then the trivia after it. That is also what makes it right for BOTH clause
// spellings — a longhand clause's span begins at the keyword the programmer wrote, and a
// bracket clause has had `requires ` prepended by `clause_text` because a bracket writes no
// keyword at all. `skip_trivia` and not a whitespace loop, because `requires // why` with the
// condition on the next line is legal and a hand-rolled skip would stop on the slashes.
function without_clause_keyword(text: String) -> String {
    let stop: Int = len(text);
    let mutable at: Int = 0;
    while at < stop && is_alpha(byte_at(text, at)) {
        at += 1;
    }
    at = skip_trivia(text, at, stop);
    return substring(text, at, stop - at);
}

// A stable insertion sort over the rendered fields of a record literal.
//
// `review.rs` calls `inner.sort()` before joining them, so that `P { a: 1, b: 2 }` and
// `P { b: 2, a: 1 }` compare EQUAL — they build the same value, so they promise the same
// thing, and a reviewer told otherwise would be reading noise. Insertion sort because the
// list is a record's fields: a handful, already nearly always in order.
function review_sort_texts(mutable xs: [String]) -> Int {
    let mutable i: Int = 1;
    while i < len(xs) {
        let held: String = xs[i];
        let mutable j: Int = i;
        while j > 0 && held < xs[j - 1] {
            xs[j] = xs[j - 1];
            j -= 1;
        }
        xs[j] = held;
        i += 1;
    }
    return len(xs);
}

// ---- reading a declaration ------------------------------------------------------------

// A token's text. Every name in this compiler is a SPAN, and a promise's name is a String
// because it is compared against another FILE's spans, where an offset means nothing.
function (self: Unit) review_token_text(src: String, token: Int) -> String {
    let t: Token = self.tokens[token];
    return substring(src, t.start, t.length);
}

// The operator, spelled as `ast.rs`'s `Display` spells it. The values are the TOKEN kinds
// `scan_punct` emitted, which is what the parser stored in the node.
pure function review_binary_op(op: Int) -> String {
    if op == 25 { return "+"; }
    if op == 26 { return "-"; }
    if op == 27 { return "*"; }
    return "/";
}

pure function review_compare_op(op: Int) -> String {
    if op == 12 { return "=="; }
    if op == 13 { return "!="; }
    if op == 14 { return "<="; }
    if op == 15 { return ">="; }
    if op == 35 { return "<"; }
    return ">";
}

pure function review_logical_op(op: Int) -> String {
    if op == 16 { return "&&"; }
    return "||";
}

// Parameter TYPES and the return type — never the parameter names, for the reason on
// `Promise.shape`.
//
// `mutable` IS part of the shape, and it was missing from both compilers. Adding it to a
// parameter changes the promise twice — the call may now change what the caller passed, and the
// caller may now only pass something writable — and this tool's whole claim is that a change
// altering no signature alters no promise. Found by sweeping the tools for A11, where
// `mutable it: dynamic Iterator<Int>` is how a driver says it advances what it is given.
// `p.b` is the flag the parser read; see `check_body`, which reads it the same way.
function (self: Unit) promise_shape(src: String, params: Int, ret: Int) -> String {
    let mutable out: String = "(";
    if params >= 0 {
        let list: Node = self.nodes[params];
        let mutable i: Int = 0;
        while i < list.b {
            if i > 0 {
                out = out + ", ";
            }
            let p: Node = self.nodes[self.children[list.a + i]];
            if p.b == 1 {
                out = out + "mutable ";
            }
            out = out + self.show_type(src, self.declared_type(src, p.a));
            i += 1;
        }
    }
    return out + ") -> " + self.show_type(src, self.declared_type(src, ret));
}

// Render an expression canonically, with parameters as POSITIONS.
//
// This is the difference between a tool a reviewer reads and one they mute. Comparing the
// clause TEXT reported `requires a <= b` as lost and `requires amount <= balance` as
// gained when a parameter was renamed — two WEAKENED lines for a change that weakened
// nothing. A tool that cries wolf on a rename teaches you to skim past the line that
// mattered.
//
// So a parameter renders by its position: `a <= b` becomes `#0 <= #1`, and the rename
// vanishes. Everything else — `self`, a field, a literal, a call — renders by name, because
// changing any of those genuinely changes the promise.
//
// A node kind this does not know about falls back to its DISCRIMINANT rather than to a
// constant, so a construct added to the language later still compares as itself. It is
// reachable only if `parse_primary` grows a kind and nobody adds a line here — the fallback
// is what keeps that a false positive instead of a contract deletion nobody was told about.
function (self: Unit) promise_render(src: String, node: Int, params: Int) -> String {
    if node < 0 {
        return "?";
    }
    let n: Node = self.nodes[node];
    if n.kind == 1 { return to_string(n.value); }
    // The unscaled integer and the scale, exactly as `review.rs` writes it: `$0.00` is
    // `0e-2`. Not a rendering of the decimal, because two spellings of the same money
    // value must compare equal and `0.10` and `0.1` do not have the same scale.
    if n.kind == 2 { return to_string(n.value) + "e-" + to_string(n.a); }
    if n.kind == 3 {
        // The source span, quotes included. See the header for where this parts company
        // with Rust's `{:?}`.
        let t: Token = self.tokens[n.token];
        return substring(src, t.start, t.length);
    }
    if n.kind == 4 {
        if n.value == 1 { return "true"; }
        return "false";
    }
    if n.kind == 5 {
        let named: Token = self.tokens[n.token];
        if params >= 0 {
            let list: Node = self.nodes[params];
            let mutable i: Int = 0;
            while i < list.b {
                let p: Node = self.nodes[self.children[list.a + i]];
                let pname: Token = self.tokens[p.token];
                if spans_equal(src, named.start, named.length, pname.start, pname.length) {
                    return "#" + to_string(i);
                }
                i += 1;
            }
        }
        return substring(src, named.start, named.length);
    }
    if n.kind == 6 { return "-(" + self.promise_render(src, n.a, params) + ")"; }
    if n.kind == 7 { return "!(" + self.promise_render(src, n.a, params) + ")"; }
    if n.kind == 8 {
        return "(" + self.promise_render(src, n.a, params) + " " + review_binary_op(n.value)
                + " " + self.promise_render(src, n.b, params) + ")";
    }
    if n.kind == 9 {
        return "(" + self.promise_render(src, n.a, params) + " " + review_compare_op(n.value)
                + " " + self.promise_render(src, n.b, params) + ")";
    }
    if n.kind == 10 {
        return "(" + self.promise_render(src, n.a, params) + " " + review_logical_op(n.value)
                + " " + self.promise_render(src, n.b, params) + ")";
    }
    if n.kind == 11 {
        return self.promise_render(src, n.a, params) + "." + self.review_token_text(src, n.token);
    }
    if n.kind == 12 {
        return self.promise_render(src, n.a, params) + "["
                + self.promise_render(src, n.b, params) + "]";
    }
    if n.kind == 13 {
        return self.review_token_text(src, n.token) + "("
                + self.promise_render_args(src, n.a, params) + ")";
    }
    if n.kind == 14 {
        return self.promise_render(src, n.a, params) + "." + self.review_token_text(src, n.token)
                + "(" + self.promise_render_args(src, n.b, params) + ")";
    }
    if n.kind == 17 {
        return "[" + self.promise_render_items(src, n.a, n.b, params) + "]";
    }
    // An interpolated string. `review.rs` renders every one as the word `interp` and so does
    // this: the pieces are expressions, so rendering them would be rendering a program, and a
    // contract clause that interpolates is not a thing anybody writes.
    if n.kind == 18 { return "interp"; }
    if n.kind == 19 {
        // A record literal, its fields SORTED — see `review_sort_texts`.
        let mutable fields: [String] = [];
        let mutable i: Int = 0;
        while i < n.b {
            let pair: Node = self.nodes[self.children[n.a + i]];
            push(fields, self.review_token_text(src, pair.token) + ": "
                    + self.promise_render(src, pair.a, params));
            i += 1;
        }
        let ordered: Int = review_sort_texts(fields);
        let mutable out: String = self.review_token_text(src, n.token) + " \{";
        i = 0;
        while i < len(fields) {
            if i > 0 {
                out = out + ", ";
            }
            out = out + fields[i];
            i += 1;
        }
        return out + "\}";
    }
    // Kind 21: the value being returned, which a bracket on the return type is about. It is
    // written nowhere in the source — that is why the node kind exists — and `parser.rs`
    // synthesizes the name `result` for the same clause, so `result` is what both render.
    if n.kind == 21 { return "result"; }
    return "node" + to_string(n.kind);
}

function (self: Unit) promise_render_args(src: String, list: Int, params: Int) -> String {
    if list < 0 {
        return "";
    }
    let n: Node = self.nodes[list];
    return self.promise_render_items(src, n.a, n.b, params);
}

function (self: Unit) promise_render_items(src: String, start: Int, count: Int,
        params: Int) -> String {
    let mutable out: String = "";
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    while i < count {
        if i > 0 {
            out = out + ", ";
        }
        out = out + self.promise_render(src, self.children[start + i], params);
        i += 1;
    }
    return out;
}

// The clause as the programmer wrote it, and WITHOUT the `requires` or `ensures` word.
//
// One line, because `emit.bx` already holds the hard half: a BRACKET clause is not one span —
// `balance: Decimal<2> [> $0.00]` has the type sitting between the subject and the clause, so
// the subject comes out of the `clause_subjects` side table and the text is two pieces joined,
// with a third case for a clause that said `it`. Reproducing that here would have been a
// second chance to get it wrong.
//
// `parser.rs` measures a longhand clause from the token AFTER the keyword and `parse_clauses`
// measures from the keyword itself, which is the only reason anything has to be stripped.
function (self: Unit) promise_clause_text(src: String, clause: Node) -> String {
    return without_clause_keyword(self.clause_text(src, clause));
}

// ---- collecting every promise a file makes --------------------------------------------

// One clause list, rendered into the two parallel tables — the comparable shape and the
// text to show — and answering how many landed.
//
// `which` is 126 for the `requires` clauses and 127 for the `ensures`. A `decreases`
// measure (128) is skipped, exactly as `review.rs` skips it: it promises that an answer
// ARRIVES rather than what the answer is, and losing one is a termination question that a
// signature comparison has nothing useful to say about.
function (mutable self: Promises) gather_clauses(unit: Unit, src: String, clauses: Int,
        params: Int, which: Int) -> Int {
    if clauses < 0 {
        return 0;
    }
    let list: Node = unit.nodes[clauses];
    let mutable found: Int = 0;
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    while i < list.b {
        let clause: Node = unit.nodes[unit.children[list.a + i]];
        if clause.value == which {
            push(self.shapes, unit.promise_render(src, clause.a, params));
            push(self.texts, unit.promise_clause_text(src, clause));
            found += 1;
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    return found;
}

// Every promise the Unit's items make, pushed into the caller's tables.
//
// Three passes, and the shape is what `collect` in `review.rs` does with its five `for`
// loops over a `Program`'s separate vectors. stage-1 has ONE item list instead, in source
// order, so the flattening is explicit:
//
//   1. every CALLABLE's item node, in one list. A free function and a method are node kinds
//      80 and 81, and an `implement` block (85) holds its methods as children — so the
//      block is opened here rather than in the loop that reads a signature, which is the
//      only reason pass 2 has no special case in it.
//   2. one Promise per callable.
//   3. the classes, their fields, and the interfaces.
//
// **Enums and `external function`s are deliberately absent**, because `review.rs` collects
// neither. An enum's promise is its variants and nothing reads them; an extern is a claim
// about a C symbol, and its `touches` is where an effect ENTERS the program rather than
// something a Burxt signature guarantees. Worth naming, because a reader who checked the
// item kinds against `parse_item` would otherwise find two missing and assume an oversight.
function (mutable self: Promises) collect(unit: Unit, src: String) -> Int {

    let mutable callables: [Int] = [];
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    while i < len(unit.items) {
        let item: Int = unit.items[i];
        let n: Node = unit.nodes[item];
        if n.kind == 80 || n.kind == 81 {
            push(callables, item);
        }
        if n.kind == 85 {
            // `class X implements Y` produces one of these with NO methods — the class's own
            // methods are what satisfy the interface — so this loop runs zero times for it,
            // and the methods are already in `unit.items` in their own right.
            let mutable j: Int = 0;
            while j < n.b {
                push(callables, unit.children[n.a + j]);
                j += 1;
            }
        }
        i += 1;
    }

    i = 0;
    while i < len(callables) {
        let n: Node = unit.nodes[callables[i]];
        // The clause node carries the return type in `c` and the receiver's token in
        // `value`, because a method item has five slots and six things to say. So a
        // function and a method are read by the same four lines.
        let clauses: Node = unit.nodes[n.c];
        let mutable name: String = unit.review_token_text(src, n.token);
        let mutable kind: String = "function";
        let mutable is_pure: Int = flag_pure(n.value);
        let mutable is_private: Int = 0;
        if n.kind == 81 {
            name = unit.review_token_text(src, clauses.value) + "." + name;
            if flag_associated(n.value) == 1 {
                // An associated function — `function open(...)` inside a class body, which is
                // how a class gets a constructor. `review.rs` finds these in `Program.fns`
                // under the qualified name `Account.open` and calls them constructors; stage-1
                // stores one as a method on the class with bit 5 set, which is the same fact
                // in two spans instead of one string.
                kind = "constructor";
            } else {
                kind = "method";
                is_private = flag_private(n.value);
                // **The zeroing is GONE, and the comment that used to be here called it.** It
                // read: *"the day `pure` methods land, `review.bx` would report a purity finding
                // `review.rs` does not"* — and narrowed to match `review.rs`, which hardcoded
                // `is_pure: false` because `MethodDef` had no field for it.
                //
                // A4 (v0.0.247) is that day. Both sides now read the real marker, in the same
                // version, because the alternative is worse than divergence: a method that STOPS
                // being `pure` is a weakened promise, and both tools would have missed it
                // silently. §C2 makes this the mechanical semver gate for 1.0, and an
                // under-reporting gate is worse than none — someone relies on it.
                //
                // So the flag is read where it always was, one line above, and nothing is zeroed.
            }
        }
        let requires_at: Int = len(self.shapes);
        let requires_count: Int = self.gather_clauses(unit, src, n.c, n.a, 126);
        let ensures_at: Int = len(self.shapes);
        let ensures_count: Int = self.gather_clauses(unit, src, n.c, n.a, 127);
        push(self.promises, Promise { name: name, kind: kind,
                        shape: unit.promise_shape(src, n.a, clauses.c),
                        requires_at: requires_at, requires_count: requires_count,
                        ensures_at: ensures_at, ensures_count: ensures_count,
                is_pure: is_pure, is_private: is_private, touches: flag_effects(n.value) });
        i += 1;
    }

    i = 0;
    while i < len(unit.items) {
        let item: Int = unit.items[i];
        let n: Node = unit.nodes[item];
        if n.kind == 82 {
            let name: String = unit.review_token_text(src, n.token);
            // The field COUNT rather than the field types, matching `review.rs`. A field's own
            // type is its own promise, one entry below, so saying it twice would double every
            // finding about a class.
            push(self.promises, Promise { name: name, kind: "class",
                            shape: to_string(n.b) + " field(s)",
                            requires_at: 0, requires_count: 0, ensures_at: 0, ensures_count: 0,
                    is_pure: 0, is_private: 0, touches: 0 });
            let mutable j: Int = 0;
            while j < n.b {
                let field: Node = unit.nodes[unit.children[n.a + j]];
                push(self.promises, Promise { name: name + "." + unit.review_token_text(src, field.token),
                                kind: "field",
                                shape: unit.show_type(src, unit.declared_type(src, field.a)),
                                requires_at: 0, requires_count: 0, ensures_at: 0, ensures_count: 0,
                        is_pure: 0, is_private: field.value, touches: 0 });
                j += 1;
            }
        }
        if n.kind == 84 {
            push(self.promises, Promise { name: unit.review_token_text(src, n.token), kind: "interface",
                            shape: to_string(n.b) + " method(s)",
                            requires_at: 0, requires_count: 0, ensures_at: 0, ensures_count: 0,
                    is_pure: 0, is_private: 0, touches: 0 });
        }
        // A `const`, and its VALUE is part of the shape — which is unusual for this file and
        // has a reason. Everywhere else the value is none of the tool's business: a body may
        // change freely as long as the signature holds. A const has no body. It is folded at
        // compile time and SUBSTITUTED into every dependent, so `const RETRIES: Int = 3;`
        // becoming `= 30` changes what a caller's compiled code does with no signature
        // anywhere altered — exactly the change this tool exists to surface.
        //
        // `params: -1`, so `promise_render` substitutes no positions and every name renders as
        // itself. That is what a const initialiser wants: a reference to another const should
        // read as that const's name.
        //
        // Rendered as WRITTEN rather than folded, matching `review.rs`, which reads
        // declarations only and has no checker to fold with. `LIMIT * 2 -> LIMIT * 3` is
        // reported, and so would a reformat of the same expression be — the one place this is
        // noisier than the rest of the file, named rather than hidden.
        if n.kind == 73 {
            push(self.promises, Promise { name: unit.review_token_text(src, n.token), kind: "const",
                            shape: unit.show_type(src, unit.declared_type(src, n.a)) + " = "
                            + unit.promise_render(src, n.b, 0 - 1),
                            requires_at: 0, requires_count: 0, ensures_at: 0, ensures_count: 0,
                    is_pure: 0, is_private: 0, touches: 0 });
        }
        i += 1;
    }

    return len(self.promises);
}

// Sort by name, then drop all but the LAST entry of each repeated name.
//
// Both halves are what `BTreeMap::insert` did for free on the Rust side, and both are
// needed. The sort is the output order — a reviewer reading two runs should not also be
// diffing the order. Keeping the last of a run is `insert`'s replace: the same key inserted
// twice leaves one value, the later one. The sort is INSERTION sort and therefore stable,
// which is what makes "the last of the run" mean "the last one collected".
//
// A program with two declarations of one name is refused by the checker, which a review
// does not run — so this is reachable only on a program that would not compile, and the
// answer it gives there is the same answer `review.rs` gives.
function review_unique_by_name(mutable promises: [Promise]) -> Int {
    let mutable i: Int = 1;
    while i < len(promises) {
        let held: Promise = promises[i];
        let mutable j: Int = i;
        while j > 0 && held.name < promises[j - 1].name {
            promises[j] = promises[j - 1];
            j -= 1;
        }
        promises[j] = held;
        i += 1;
    }
    // Compact in place, then cut the tail off: `truncate` is the only way to shorten a
    // growable array, and writing the survivors forward means one pass and no second array.
    let mutable kept: Int = 0;
    i = 0;
    while i < len(promises) {
        // `||` short-circuits, which is what makes `promises[i + 1]` safe on the last entry —
        // the bound is tested first and the index is never evaluated. Written as one condition
        // rather than a nested `if` because the two halves are one question.
        let last_of_run: Bool = i + 1 >= len(promises) || promises[i].name != promises[i + 1].name;
        if last_of_run {
            promises[kept] = promises[i];
            kept += 1;
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    let dropped: Int = truncate(promises, kept);
    return kept;
}

// A file's promises: the module loader, then the lexer, then the parser, then the walk.
//
// `load_program` and not `read_file`, so `use "..."` is followed exactly as a compile would
// follow it. A review that read only the named file would miss a contract deleted in
// another one, which is the failure mode a semver gate can least afford.
//
// No typechecker — see the header for why that is what keeps this agreeing with `review.rs`
// rather than merely being cheap.
//
// Answers 0 when it read the file and -1 when it could not, which is what a Burxt function has
// instead of `Result<_, String>`: there is no generic `Result` yet (`ROADMAP-1.0.md` A3), and a
// one-off enum for a value tested on the next line would be worse than a checked Int.
//
// **The `Unit` literal is spelled out here**, and there is exactly one of it in this file even
// though a review parses two programs — because this function is CALLED twice and each call
// builds its own. A helper returning one was written first and the compiler refused it,
// *"cannot return Unit, because its storage lives in a region and would not outlive it"*, which
// is the region rule doing its job. `lsp.bx` needs two Units at two call sites and therefore
// carries the literal twice; a review's second Unit falls out of the second call, so one copy
// is enough.
//
// It is still a copy of `main.bx`'s, and that part is a defect rather than a design: `Unit` has
// a hundred and twelve fields, so a field added to `ast.bx` has to be added everywhere the
// literal appears or one of them stops compiling. Since a class cannot be returned, the fix is
// not a constructor function — it is `Unit` growing a method that fills a blank one, or the
// arrays moving behind something that can be. Reported rather than worked around.
function promises_of(path: String, mutable into: Promises) -> Int touches files, input {
    let src: String = load_program(path, std_roots());
    let mutable unit: Unit = Unit { pending_public: 0, package_names: [], package_starts: [], package_ends: [], private_names: [], private_owners: [], tokens: [], keywords: [], nodes: [], children: [], nested_children: [], items: [], bindings: [], consts: [], functions: [], stack: [], methods: [], types: [], params_in_scope: [], generics: [], type_parameters: [], type_param_bounds: [], pending_bounds: [], current_generic: 0 - 1, current_method: 0 - 1, probing: 0, probe_changes: 0, grow_parameters: [], grow_self: [], relay_parameters: [], relay_self: [], relay_method_parameters: [], relay_aliases: [], relay_ceiling: 0, type_bindings: [], measure_clause: 0 - 1, measure_slot: 0, measure_parameters: 0 - 1, measure_owner: "", old_nodes: [], old_slots: [], ensures_start: 0, ensures_count: 0, ensures_owner: "", bracket_clauses: [], clause_subjects: [], it_token: 0 - 1, it_is_result: 0, used_it: 0, it_seen: 0, result_type_node: 0 - 1, result_slot: 0 - 1, returns_node: 0 - 1, walked_types: [], type_binding_floor: 0, binding_floor: 0, allowed_effects: 63, effects_owner: "", function_buckets: [], function_chain: [], instances: [], instance_arguments: [], impls: [], impl_traits: [], typed: [], region_depth: 0, region_token: 0 - 1, in_caller_region: 0, in_pure: 0, loop_depth: 0, in_contract: 0, in_ensures: 0, current_function: -1, chunks: [], pending: "", globals: "", global_chunks: [], sret: "", sret_cells: 0, function_entry: "", body_chunks: [], body_pending: "", next_register: 0, next_slot: 0, next_label: 0, slots: [], strings: [], declared: [], loop_condition_labels: [], loop_end_labels: [], region_marks: [], loop_region_depths: [], block_release: [], placing: 0, rel_frames: [], rel_names: [], in_function: 0, depth: 0, param_depth: 0 - 1, problems: 0, diagnose: 0, problem_start: 0 - 1, problem_length: 0, problem_message: "", position: 0, token_end: 0, no_record_literal: 0, in_extern_signature: 0, range_bounds: 0, in_multiply: 0, print_to_stderr: 0, int_type_node: 0 - 1, errors: 0, type_of_calls: 0, find_binding_calls: 0, find_function_calls: 0, show_type_calls: 0, expr_allocates_calls: 0, type_at_calls: 0 };
    let installed: Int = unit.install_keywords();
    let total: Int = unit.run(src);
    if unit.errors > 0 {
        return 0 - 1;
    }
    while !unit.done() {
        let item: Int = unit.parse_item(src);
        if item < 0 {
            return 0 - 1;
        }
        push(unit.items, item);
    }
    if unit.errors > 0 {
        return 0 - 1;
    }
    let collected: Int = into.collect(unit, src);
    let unique: Int = review_unique_by_name(into.promises);
    return 0;
}

// ---- comparing ------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Where `name` is, or -1. Linear, over a list that is sorted — a binary search is the
// obvious improvement and it is not taken, for the reason every other lookup in this
// compiler is a scan: a program has tens of declarations, the scan is what the sort is
// already paying for, and `find_binding` and `find_type` set the precedent. If a review of
// something enormous ever measures slow, `function_buckets` in `ast.bx` is the pattern to
// copy.
function find_promise(among: Promises, name: String) -> Int {
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    while i < len(among.promises) {
        if among.promises[i].name == name {
            return i;
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    return 0 - 1;
}

// Does this clause list still hold a clause with the same comparable shape?
//
// The SHAPE and not the text, which is the whole of the rename fix — see `promise_render`.
function review_has_clause(shapes: [String], at: Int, count: Int, shape: String) -> Bool {
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    while i < count {
        if shapes[at + i] == shape {
            return true;
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    return false;
}

// Everything two versions of one declaration disagree about.
//
// The ORDER the findings are raised in is part of the output, because the final sort is by
// verdict and name only — so two WEAKENED findings about one declaration come out in
// exactly the order they are pushed here. It is `review.rs`'s order, deliberately: lost
// clauses, gained clauses, gained effects, lost effects, purity, privacy, shape.
function compare_promises(was: Promise, before: Promises, now: Promise, after: Promises,
        mutable out: [Finding]) -> Int {
    // A contract that was there and is not is THE finding this tool exists for.
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    while i < was.requires_count {
        if !review_has_clause(after.shapes, now.requires_at, now.requires_count,
                before.shapes[was.requires_at + i]) {
            push(out, Finding { verdict: WEAKENED, name: was.name,
                    detail: "lost `requires " + before.texts[was.requires_at + i] + "`" });
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    i = 0;
    while i < was.ensures_count {
        if !review_has_clause(after.shapes, now.ensures_at, now.ensures_count,
                before.shapes[was.ensures_at + i]) {
            push(out, Finding { verdict: WEAKENED, name: was.name,
                    detail: "lost `ensures " + before.texts[was.ensures_at + i] + "`" });
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    i = 0;
    while i < now.requires_count {
        if !review_has_clause(before.shapes, was.requires_at, was.requires_count,
                after.shapes[now.requires_at + i]) {
            push(out, Finding { verdict: STRICTER, name: was.name,
                    detail: "gained `requires " + after.texts[now.requires_at + i] + "`" });
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    i = 0;
    while i < now.ensures_count {
        if !review_has_clause(before.shapes, was.ensures_at, was.ensures_count,
                after.shapes[now.ensures_at + i]) {
            push(out, Finding { verdict: STRICTER, name: was.name,
                    detail: "gained `ensures " + after.texts[now.ensures_at + i] + "`" });
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    // An effect GAINED is the change a reviewer most wants surfaced: this function can now
    // reach something it could not. Because effects are DECLARED rather than inferred
    // (v0.0.159), an agent cannot add one without changing a signature — which is what
    // makes this reportable at all.
    //
    // Bit order, low to high, is `Effect`'s `Ord` order in `ast.rs` and therefore the order
    // the sorted Rust `Vec` is walked in. See `Promise.touches`.
    let mutable bit: Int = 1;
    while bit <= 32 {
        if bit_and(now.touches, bit) == bit && bit_and(was.touches, bit) == 0 {
            push(out, Finding { verdict: WEAKENED, name: was.name,
                    detail: "now touches " + effect_name(bit) + " — it could not before" });
        }
        bit = bit * 2;
    }
    bit = 1;
    while bit <= 32 {
        if bit_and(was.touches, bit) == bit && bit_and(now.touches, bit) == 0 {
            push(out, Finding { verdict: STRICTER, name: was.name,
                    detail: "no longer touches " + effect_name(bit) });
        }
        bit = bit * 2;
    }
    if was.is_pure == 1 && now.is_pure == 0 {
        push(out, Finding { verdict: WEAKENED, name: was.name,
                        detail: "no longer `pure` — its answer may now depend on more than its "
                + "arguments" });
    }
    if was.is_pure == 0 && now.is_pure == 1 {
        push(out, Finding { verdict: STRICTER, name: was.name,
                detail: "now `pure`" });
    }
    // Privacy is a promise about who may touch this. Opening it up is a weakening of exactly
    // the same kind as dropping a precondition: something that could not happen now can.
    if was.is_private == 1 && now.is_private == 0 {
        push(out, Finding { verdict: WEAKENED, name: was.name,
                detail: "no longer `private` — anything may now read it" });
    }
    if was.is_private == 0 && now.is_private == 1 {
        push(out, Finding { verdict: STRICTER, name: was.name,
                detail: "now `private`" });
    }
    if was.shape != now.shape {
        push(out, Finding { verdict: CHANGED, name: was.name,
                detail: was.shape + " -> " + now.shape });
    }
    return len(out);
}

// Weakenings first, then by name — and STABLE, so that the findings about one declaration
// keep the order `compare_promises` raised them in. `review.rs` relies on the same property
// of `sort_by`; an unstable sort would reorder two WEAKENED lines about one function and the
// two implementations would stop agreeing on a program where nothing is wrong.
function review_sort_findings(mutable xs: [Finding]) -> Int {
    let mutable i: Int = 1;
    while i < len(xs) {
        let held: Finding = xs[i];
        let held_rank: Int = verdict_rank(held.verdict);
        let mutable j: Int = i;
        // `while` and not a `for`, and the condition is spelled out rather than folded into a
        // comparison helper, because Burxt has no function values: a comparator would have to
        // be a name this file could not pass anywhere.
        let mutable moving: Bool = true;
        while moving && j > 0 {
            let ahead: Finding = xs[j - 1];
            let ahead_rank: Int = verdict_rank(ahead.verdict);
            if ahead_rank > held_rank || (ahead_rank == held_rank && ahead.name > held.name) {
                xs[j] = ahead;
                j -= 1;
            } else {
                moving = false;
            }
        }
        xs[j] = held;
        i += 1;
    }
    return len(xs);
}

// ---- the tool -------------------------------------------------------------------------

// What changed about what these two programs PROMISE. Answers the exit code, so a caller is
// a two-line CLI branch: `0` nothing weakened, `1` something weakened, `2` unreadable.
//
// The exit code is part of the contract and not a convenience. It is what makes this usable
// as a CI gate with no output parsing, which is what `ROADMAP-1.0.md` §C2 asks of it.
function review(old_path: String, new_path: String) -> Int touches files, input {
    // The two promise tables belong to THIS frame, because a class holding growable arrays
    // cannot be returned out of one — see `Promises`.
    let mutable before: Promises = Promises { promises: [], shapes: [], texts: [] };
    if promises_of(old_path, before) < 0 {
        print_error("error: " + old_path + " could not be read or parsed");
        return 2;
    }
    let mutable after: Promises = Promises { promises: [], shapes: [], texts: [] };
    if promises_of(new_path, after) < 0 {
        print_error("error: " + new_path + " could not be read or parsed");
        return 2;
    }

    let mutable findings: [Finding] = [];
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    while i < len(before.promises) {
        let was: Promise = before.promises[i];
        let at: Int = find_promise(after, was.name);
        if at < 0 {
            // A removed declaration is a weakening only if something still calls it, which
            // this cannot know — so it is reported plainly and left to the reviewer.
            push(findings, Finding { verdict: REMOVED, name: was.name,
                    detail: was.kind + " is gone" });
        } else {
            let compared: Int = compare_promises(was, before, after.promises[at], after,
                    findings);
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    i = 0;
    while i < len(after.promises) {
        let now: Promise = after.promises[i];
        if find_promise(before, now.name) < 0 {
            let mutable detail: String = "new " + now.kind;
            if now.is_private == 1 {
                detail = detail + ", private";
            }
            push(findings, Finding { verdict: ADDED, name: now.name,
                    detail: detail });
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    let ordered: Int = review_sort_findings(findings);

    if len(findings) == 0 {
        print("no promise changed.");
        print("");
        print("Every signature is identical, so whatever moved in the bodies, this program");
        print("guarantees exactly what it guaranteed before.");
        return 0;
    }

    let mutable weakened: Int = 0;
    i = 0;
    while i < len(findings) {
        if findings[i].verdict == WEAKENED {
            weakened += 1;
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    i = 0;
    while i < len(findings) {
        let f: Finding = findings[i];
        // `{:<9} {:<34} {}` in `review.rs`. Three columns and two literal spaces, so a
        // verdict longer than nine or a name longer than thirty-four pushes its neighbour
        // right rather than being cut — a truncated qualified name would be a lie about
        // which declaration changed.
        print(review_pad(f.verdict, 9) + " " + review_pad(f.name, 34) + " " + f.detail);
        i += 1;
    }
    print("");
    if weakened > 0 {
        print(to_string(weakened) + " weakened promise(s). A weakened contract is the one "
                + "change that passes every test — the tests were failing BECAUSE of it.");
        // Non-zero, so this is usable as a gate in CI without parsing the output.
        return 1;
    }
    print(to_string(len(findings)) + " change(s), none weakening.");
    return 0;
}
